# Line endings are a property of the project, not of whoever cloned it.
#
# Without this file, normalisation comes from each contributor's global git config —
# on Windows that is usually core.autocrlf=true, which is why every commit from a
# Windows machine emits "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings. Every tracked text
# blob in this repo is already stored LF, so `text=auto` changes nothing on disk; it
# just makes that the rule rather than an accident.
* text=auto

# NOT eol=crlf on the ModsConfig fixture, deliberately, in case anyone is tempted:
# RimWorld's file is CRLF, but the fixture is only ever PARSED by the tests, never
# byte-compared. The byte-exact guard (ModsConfigWriterTests) builds its expected
# string from explicit \r\n escapes in C# source, so it is independent of how any
# file on disk is stored — which is what makes it correct on Linux and macOS too.

*.cs        text diff=csharp
*.csproj    text
*.slnx      text
*.axaml     text
*.xml       text
*.json      text
*.md        text
*.yml       text
*.py        text
*.sh        text eol=lf

# Binary: never normalise, never diff as text.
*.png       binary
*.ico       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.svg       text
*.dll       binary
*.exe       binary
*.zip       binary
*.db        binary

# Screenshots are documentation, not source; keep them out of language statistics.
assets/screenshots/** linguist-documentation
